# 2026-08-31 - Kokoro TTS: measurement, then the engine layer
Machine: Windows 11, 14 physical / 20 logical cores, CPU only (no GPU used at any point).
Python 3.13.3, onnxruntime 1.29.0 (CPU build), kokoro-onnx 0.6.1.

## 1. Why fp32 and not the "small" int8 model
Same sentences, same voice (af_heart), warm-up call excluded.

  model  threads  load   short sentence           long sentence            RTF
  fp32   4        0.81s  0.70s / 1.81s audio      1.73s / 5.53s audio      0.38 / 0.31
  fp32   8        0.86s  0.51s / 1.81s audio      1.31s / 5.53s audio      0.28 / 0.24
  int8   4        0.71s  1.69s / 1.88s audio      4.43s / 5.59s audio      0.90 / 0.79
  int8   8        0.75s  1.81s / 1.88s audio      4.62s / 5.59s audio      0.97 / 0.83

fp32 is 3.4x FASTER than int8 here. The 92MB build is a trap: dynamic
quantization has no fast kernel on this CPU, so it trades 233MB of disk for
most of the speed. int8 at RTF ~0.9 cannot stay ahead of the speaker.

## 2. Thread count (int8 sweep, before fp32 was tried)
  threads=default  RTF 0.93
  threads=4        RTF 0.82
  threads=8        RTF 0.82
  threads=14       RTF 0.95
  threads=20       RTF 1.21   <- oversubscribed, synthesis falls behind speech

8 is the default. Using all 20 logical cores is SLOWER than using 8.

## 3. End to end, through SpeechPlayer -> build_engine("auto") -> Kokoro
Real model, real settings, markdown reply with a fenced code block and a link.

  engine selected by auto: kokoro
  chunk 1: ready at 2.13s, 4.14s of audio
  chunk 2: ready at 2.53s, 1.34s of audio
  chunk 3: ready at 3.40s, 3.73s of audio
  chunk 4: ready at 3.81s, 1.45s of audio
  chunk 5: ready at 4.26s, 1.64s of audio
  total synth 4.26s for 12.31s of speech -> RTF 0.35

The producer stays ~3 sentences ahead of playback, so the voice never pauses
mid-paragraph. The code block was replaced with "code block omitted" and the
markdown link read as its text, as prepare_spoken_text intends.

## 4. Latency as actually felt, same player, three replies
  cold (model load)  first audio at 1.65s
  warm               first audio at 0.60s
  warm               first audio at 0.85s

The model loads once per session. Every reply after that starts speaking in
under a second.

## 5. CPU-only, verified rather than assumed
  providers=['CPUExecutionProvider']

kokoro-onnx's own resolve_providers() returns EVERY available provider once an
accelerated onnxruntime is installed, which would hand a spoken reply the GPU
that Ollama is using. The session is therefore built here with the provider
named explicitly, and test_kokoro_never_asks_onnxruntime_for_the_gpu pins it.

## 6. Tests
  .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest tests/test_voice_engines.py -q
  17 passed

  .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest tests/ -q
  4212 passed, 2 skipped, 1 warning in 579.28s

One defect was found by the tests rather than by listening: after stop(), the
consumer kept yielding audio that was already queued behind the producer. The
real player breaks on the flag so playback was correct, but an engine that has
been told to stop must not hand out sound at all. Fixed in _synthesize_ahead.

## 7. Samples to listen to
~/.shamsu/voices/samples/  - af_heart, af_bella, am_michael, am_puck, bf_emma,
bm_george, and reply-as-spoken.wav (the markdown reply above, as SHAMSU says it).
